\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The corpus by first collection query and by publication year. The sample follows search relevance and does not represent research output as a whole.}}{8}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Architecture. Public data is cleaned and cached, the mining core serves the workspace, and the experiment scripts write the artifacts used in this report. Solr is an optional backend.}}{9}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Search view with query, filters, lexical and graph ranking, over real papers in the corpus.}}{10}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Citation network view. Each edge is an observed reference and points from the citing paper to the cited paper.}}{11}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces A project from the browser test. The example brief can be edited; papers and edges come from the corpus. Node positions are a layout and do not encode semantic distance.}}{12}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The original PDF at bookmarked page 4, next to the reader's notes. The note text is from the demo and is not used as an experimental label.}}{12}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Records in the conference catalog after deduplication, by conference and year. The catalog is kept apart from the 3,595 paper corpus used in the experiments.}}{13}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces A real search of the conference catalog: the query ``graph neural networks'' filtered to ICML 2025. Missing abstracts and citation counts are shown as missing.}}{14}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Online search over live OpenAlex metadata. An imported paper can be read, added to a project and joined to the citation graph when its references match papers in the corpus. No edge is created without a reference in the source.}}{15}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces The 160 papers with the highest PageRank and the observed citations among them. Node area encodes PageRank and color encodes the Louvain community; three titles are labeled.}}{17}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Real PeerRead reviews on the left; analysis of a paragraph typed by the user on the right. The typed paragraph is a demo input and is not in the benchmark.}}{18}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces The personal library with BibTeX export and the controls for the recommendation method and diversity.}}{19}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces E3. Left: nDCG@10 of the five methods with 95\% bootstrap intervals. Right: nDCG@10 against diversity within the list; marker area encodes catalog coverage.}}{22}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces nDCG@10 of every recommender of the app and of the logistic regression reranker on E2 and E2b (Table~\ref {tab:author}). E2b is harder for every method, and the order of the citation signals stays the same.}}{24}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Fusion, the decision model and the decision model with a topic on the three tasks. Left: nDCG@10. Right: Hit@10.}}{26}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Language models as rankers on the same 20 (left) and 100 (right) fusion candidates, with fusion and the decision model reranking the same candidates.}}{28}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Quality against cost. Each point is a ranker; the vertical axis is the mean nDCG@10 over the three tasks and the horizontal axis the median time per request on a log scale. The number in parentheses is the candidate count.}}{30}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Left: nDCG@10 on SciFact with 95\% bootstrap intervals over queries. Right: macro F1 of the three review polarity methods with 95\% intervals from bootstrap samples over test papers.}}{31}{figure.caption.57}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Confusion matrix of the logistic regression on the test reviews. The mixed label does not correspond to one kind of review sentence.}}{31}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces The reader selects a sentence about TransE and TransH. The first result quotes a sentence about how such models represent relations as translations.}}{37}{figure.caption.63}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Finding abstracts with the same method name on SciERC. Both rows use the same 172 sentences and candidates; the lower row replaces the technique name in the query with a neutral phrase.}}{39}{figure.caption.67}%
